window.DEVLOG_INDEX = [{"t": "The crew hit the usage limit and went dark for two hours", "s": "Three hours into the jam the whole crew ran out of usage. A 19-minute warning meant nothing was lost, but it cost two hours, a filming block, and a restart that failed the first time.", "u": "posts/2026-11-12-1030-two-hours-dark.html", "c": ["Bloopers", "Behind the scenes"], "w": ["Director", "Engine", "Me"], "d": "Day 1, hour 6", "o": 1794479400.0, "i": "media/usage-stall-timeline.thumb.jpg", "x": "Eleven Claude Code sessions working at once use a lot. A little after 08:00, three hours into the jam, I could see the usage limit coming, and told the Director. The stall on a timeline. No commits from 08:26 to 10:20, and the 09:30 filming block fell in the middle. The warning The Director mailed every session at 08:06, marked urgent: \"Right now, before your next step: (1) commit everything you have in your own paths (work in progress is fine: say WIP in the message) … (2) add a note to your current task saying exactly where you are and the next step … (3) then carry on working as normal until the limit stops you. Do NOT park or pause for this.\" The Engine got its own line: leave the editor running, and if a merge is half done, \"finish or revert to a green build first\". That last part mattered. At 08:02 the main build had broken: two gameplay sessions had each defined the same struct on their own branches. The fix was merged at 08:11, with the limit minutes away. The crew stopped on a build that compiles. The stop The last commit landed at 08:25. Then nothing for 1 hour 56 minutes. What it cost: Two hours of a 93-hour jam , for every session at once. The 09:30 filming block. 22 finished tasks were already queued for the Engine to film. They're still waiting, so there's no new footage in this post. What it didn't cost: any work. Every session had committed and left a note for whoever picked the task up next. Back on, the second time At 10:20 the limit reset and I told the Dir"}, {"t": "The first replay is a rough cut", "s": "The reel recorder replayed its first take with kills: three puppets, 16 shots cut between the stage's camera marks. About one shot in three came out black.", "u": "posts/2026-11-12-0810-first-replay-rough-cut.html", "c": ["Gameplay", "Bloopers"], "w": ["Gameplay", "Engine"], "d": "Day 1, hour 4", "o": 1794471000.0, "i": "media/reel-first-replay-rough-cut.thumb.jpg", "x": "The game is named after this feature. You play a scene in first person; when you clear it, the reel plays your take back as a film, cut between cameras placed on the set. Seven seconds of the first replay with kills. The figure is the player, replayed as a puppet. It cuts to a wall, then to black, then to the shot where two test dummies are knocked over. This is the first time the second Gameplay session's recorder ran on a take with kills in it. It records every actor and every event, then builds an edit list before playback: a wide opening, then cuts to whichever camera mark has the best view about half a second before each kill. The Engine's log from this take: \"ReplayStarted (35.42s, 3 puppets, 16 shots, 4 camera candidates)\" Me, from outside. In play this is a first-person game; the replay rebuilds the player as a third-person puppet from the recording. The kill shot: both dummy boxes tipping over, seen from a camera mark across the stage. What's wrong with it Plenty, and the Engine's capture note lists it: Black shots. About one in three. Probably a camera mark aimed at the unlit side of the stage, or a camera inside a wall. No orbit. The last kill should slow down with the camera circling it. It doesn't show. Nobody fought. There was no hit command yet, so the Engine killed the dummies from Python, about three seconds apart. The 35-second take is mostly the Engine waiting on its own tool calls. The time rule bit again The first attempt replayed for a second and a half."}, {"t": "The first zombie walks, lunges and ragdolls", "s": "The NPC session's shambler walks at the camera, shows a tell, lunges, and ragdolls from one hit. Its two faster cousins stood still in their own capture.", "u": "posts/2026-11-12-0755-first-zombie.html", "c": ["Characters and NPCs", "Bloopers"], "w": ["NPC AI", "Engine"], "d": "Day 1, hour 3", "o": 1794470100.0, "i": "media/shambler-approach-lunge-ragdoll.thumb.jpg", "x": "The first enemy is a zombie shambler. It's also the pattern for every enemy after it: one enemy Blueprint, set up by a data asset per type. A shambler spawns 7 metres out, walks in, tells, and hop-lunges into the camera. Then a fresh one takes a debug hit and ragdolls away. It wears the plain Unreal mannequin for now. The looks come later; today is about the behaviour: walk at the player, a tell of about 0.6 seconds, a lunge that kills if it connects, and one hit to put it down. The approach. The yellow ring and the label over its head are debug drawing. The lunge, from the receiving end. One hit: the ragdoll is thrown the way the hit was going. Not the plan: no StateTree The task asked for the behaviour as a StateTree, Unreal's state machine asset, built by a kit script. Unreal 5.9 is a preview, and the NPC session found the scripting side isn't there yet. Its own line: \"It's a small C++ state machine on game time, because 5.9's StateTree scripting can't author transitions yet.\" It has the same five states the task listed: idle, approach, telegraph, attack, dead. \"On game time\" matters here. Every timing slows down with the world when the player stands still. Too slow to film That caused the first problem in the filming block. The time system was merged by then, and the camera doesn't move. The Engine's capture note: \"Standing still made the world crawl so slowly that the placed shambler barely moved in 30 s, so I forced 'rt.Time.Set 0.4'.\" So this clip runs at 40% speed. It"}, {"t": "Run, slide, wall kick: the player moves", "s": "The Gameplay session's first-person character runs, slides under a gap and kicks off a wall, with the camera feel driven by one data asset. Filmed in the 07:30 block.", "u": "posts/2026-11-12-0750-run-slide-wall-kick.html", "c": ["Gameplay"], "w": ["Gameplay", "Engine"], "d": "Day 1, hour 3", "o": 1794469800.0, "i": "media/movement-run-slide-wall-kick.thumb.jpg", "x": "Movement was the top-priority task of day one. Reel Time's world only runs while you move, so moving has to feel good before anything else matters. Five seconds on the soundstage: run, slide under the lintel, run at the checker wall, jump, kick off it. The yellow line is the debug readout: speed, state, kicks used, field of view, camera roll. This is our own character, not the template one. The Gameplay session had it written and passing headless tests 32 minutes after the task was created. It then waited for the Engine session to merge it and film it, which is how every piece of the game reaches the screen. Mid-slide under the lintel. The field of view widens and the camera rolls into the slide. Full run at the checker wall the demo spawns, 8.2 metres a second. Just after the kick. The camera rolls away from the wall, then settles. Every number in one place The task asked for a camera that feels \"heavy but never nauseating\", with every value in a data asset. A kit script writes that asset, so tuning needs no rebuild. Some of what's in it now: Run: 8.2 m/s. Slide: needs 4.5 m/s to start, adds a boost up to 14.5 m/s, lasts a second at most. Wall kick: pushes away and up, three per jump, and never twice in a row off the same wall. Camera: field of view 95°, up to 14° wider with speed and 8° more in a slide. Roll of 7° into a slide and 11° away from a kicked wall. Every camera effect is multiplied by a comfort scale from 0 to 1, which the settings menu will drive. What you're no"}, {"t": "Two more sets, and one that built itself three times", "s": "A gas station and a 1950s main street went up as greybox sets from kit scripts in the first filming block. The gas station turned out to hold three copies of itself.", "u": "posts/2026-11-12-0740-two-more-sets.html", "c": ["World and visuals", "Bloopers"], "w": ["World", "Engine", "Floater"], "d": "Day 1, hour 3", "o": 1794469200.0, "i": "media/millbrook-main-street-greybox-flythrough.thumb.jpg", "x": "Reel Time is a run of film sets, one per invented B-movie. This morning's first filming block caught two of them standing: a zombie picture and a flying-saucer picture, both still in greybox. Saucers Over Millbrook, Scene 4: a fly up the main street, past the parked cars to the crashed saucer, then up to the one still hovering. Saucers Over Millbrook A 1950s main street at night, 52 by 30 metres, with stage walls round it. The enemies in this film will fire slow plasma bolts you can see coming, so the street is built as lanes of fire: long sight lines to cross, parked cars to slide behind, and red walls in an alley to kick between when you need to change lane. Where the player starts. Pastel shopfronts, staggered cars for cover, the crashed saucer closing the far end and a second one overhead. The wide camera mark. The hotel balcony on the left is where two enemies will shoot down the street. The overhead mark. The red disc by the pink diner is the mark the player finishes on. Dead Mile Motel A roadside gas station, 44 by 40 metres, fenced all round. You start on the road looking straight at the first two zombies under the canopy lights, because the player reads the room while time is stopped. The red blocks are pumps and barrels that will explode later. Dead Mile Motel, Scene 3: from the player start, up the road under the pump canopy, to the shop door and over the roof. The player start. Four red pump placeholders under the canopy, the shop door lit behind them. The low cam"}, {"t": "The look arrives: production paperwork, in 19 artboards", "s": "I ran the UI brief through Claude Design and got a style tile, a logo, a component sheet and 15 screens. Menus are call sheets, the pause screen is a slate that says HOLD.", "u": "posts/2026-11-12-0710-ui-direction.html", "c": ["Interface"], "w": ["Me", "UI"], "d": "Day 1, hour 2", "o": 1794466680.0, "i": "media/ui-mockup-call-sheet-menu.thumb.jpg", "x": "While the crew built systems, I took the UI brief that was written before the jam and ran it through Claude Design. It came back as 19 artboards. My verdict to the Director: \"Subject to change but a decent start\" These are mockups, not screenshots. Nothing here is in the game yet, and the film titles on them are placeholders, not the game's titles. The idea: paperwork meets cinema Every screen is something you'd find on a film set. The main menu is a call sheet on a clipboard. The level list is a shooting schedule. Pausing drops a slate that says HOLD. Finishing a scene deals you a dailies card with your take count stamped on it. The style tile: paper cream, ink black, tape red, and a pair of accent colours for each film genre. Four typefaces do all the work: Anton for the slams, Bebas Neue for labels, Courier Prime for body text and Caveat for the director's handwritten notes. One rule I like: grain and misprinting only on the big slams. Text you have to read mid-fight stays clean. The logo Three logo directions. A was chosen: the doubled E of REEL as a frame of film. The HUD, in three films The HUD is the same parts in every level, recoloured by the film you're in: a slate in the corner with the scene and take, and a film strip along the bottom whose sprockets only move as fast as time does. HUD mockup for the zombie film: orange accents, shells for ammo. HUD mockup for the 50s alien film: teal and pink, plasma cells. HUD mockup for the noir film: black and white only, so w"}, {"t": "The first set, and a film look that won't hold still", "s": "Two hours in, there's a soundstage built by a script and a first-pass worn-film look on top of it. The look is meant to freeze when time stops. It doesn't yet.", "u": "posts/2026-11-12-0700-first-set-first-look.html", "c": ["World and visuals", "Bloopers"], "w": ["World", "Engine"], "d": "Day 1, hour 2", "o": 1794466320.0, "i": "media/grindhouse-look-pass1.thumb.jpg", "x": "This is the first picture of Reel Time that isn't a diagram or Epic's template. It's the screen-test soundstage, where the game will teach you that time only runs while you move, seen through the first pass of the zombie theme's film look. Pass one of the grindhouse look on the screen-test set: grain, scratches, a vignette, frame jitter and a warm cast. The set The World session doesn't place anything by hand. It wrote a small library of soundstage pieces (box, wall, ramp, stairs, balcony, plywood flat, light stand, camera mark) and a kit script that builds the level from them. The Engine session runs the kit in the open editor. Run it again and it wipes its own pieces and rebuilds, so the layout lives in a text file that can be reviewed and changed. What's in the room: a low wall with a red lintel to slide under, a taped X to finish on, two tall red walls to kick between, stairs and platforms for height, and one plywood flat with its bracing showing, because every set in this game is a set. Before After The same camera with the look off and on. Whites go cream, reds go orange, the corners darken. The look, honestly It reads as old film. It doesn't read as 1970s grindhouse yet: the cast is closer to sepia than to the saturated, blown-out colour of exploitation prints, and it turns the darker corners to brown mush. That's a tuning job. The bigger miss is the point of the whole effect. The look is supposed to run on time speed: grain and scratches move while the reel runs, and "}, {"t": "First footage, and the take that filmed the wrong window", "s": "The Engine session proved the whole pipeline in 11 minutes: kits, live C++ compiles, DLSS and filming the game by itself. Its first clip recorded the wrong window.", "u": "posts/2026-11-12-0640-first-footage.html", "c": ["Tools and pipeline", "Bloopers"], "w": ["Engine"], "d": "Day 1, hour 2", "o": 1794465360.0, "i": "media/first-footage-template-map.thumb.jpg", "x": "This isn't the game yet. It's Unreal's stock first-person template, driven by injected input, filmed by the Engine session with nobody at the keyboard. Nothing in this clip will ship. What matters is that the crew can now run something, move in it and film it on its own, which is how every later task gets checked. Three cuts from the Engine session's test clip: walk to the cylinder, turn, walk the wall. The input is scripted. Take one The first clip didn't show the game at all. The recorder had grabbed a different window on my desktop. The Engine session spotted it, deleted the clip and its contact sheets, and sent the Devlog and the Director an urgent message before anything was copied: \"If you copied any of them (devlog repo, drafts, site), delete them and don't publish.\" Nothing had been. The capture setup now records the game window directly, the clip was re-shot three minutes later, and the Toolsmith was asked to fix the recording script so it can't happen again. Before this clip went on the site, a reviewer checked all 872 frames for anything that wasn't the game. That's the \"don't suffer silently\" rule doing its job in hour 2: say what went wrong at once, fix it, write it down. What the baseline check proved The Engine session's first task: six checks, two with caveats. Task T-0001 took 11 minutes, from 06:08 to 06:19. The summary in the Engine's own words: \"Baseline PASS with caveats.\" Kits work. A throwaway kit created one material in the running editor and was delet"}, {"t": "Pitch locked: Reel Time, nearly three hours early", "s": "The first milestone landed at 06:08, 68 minutes into the jam. Minutes later the Director had five contracts and twelve tasks written and five sessions building.", "u": "posts/2026-11-12-0615-pitch-locked.html", "c": ["Milestones", "Behind the scenes"], "w": ["Me", "Director"], "d": "Day 1, hour 2", "o": 1794464100.0, "i": "media/pitch-locked-slate.thumb.jpg", "x": "The first milestone, \"Pitch locked\", was due at 09:00. It was done at 06:08, in hour 2. It went that fast because the thinking was already done. Wednesday's Q&A had become a kickoff brief (see Pre-production ). This morning the Director read it, drafted the design overview, and read back four points for me to confirm. My whole answer: \"1, if you think ours is gonna be better with more control go for it. 2 correct. 3 yes, 4 i would like to playtest that this evening. [...] but for now, go\" What that decided The pitch is locked as written, under the working title Reel Time. The replay is our own system. Unreal's built-in replay records network replication, reloads the level to play back, and doesn't replay ragdolls or heavy time scaling faithfully. Ours records each take as samples and events, then replays it with puppets and an automatic edit. DLSS ships as a settings dropdown, and we go for the NVIDIA RTX Award as a bonus. The port was already done. I playtest the greybox loop this evening. Contracts before code Before any code existed, the Director wrote five short contracts: events, the reel, levels, NPCs and UI data. Each says who provides a system and what everyone else can build against, so gameplay, world and the rest can start in parallel instead of waiting for each other. The Director logged this one as an assumption, not a decision: the gameplay session may change names as it builds, as long as it reports them. The rule at the centre of the events contract: Every cha"}, {"t": "Porting NVIDIA DLSS from Unreal 5.8 to 5.9 in 75 minutes", "s": "NVIDIA's DLSS 4.5 plugin stops at Unreal 5.8. Six compiles, three editor launches and one crash later it runs on our 5.9 engine, without Frame Generation.", "u": "posts/2026-11-12-0605-dlss-on-unreal-5-9.html", "c": ["Tools and pipeline", "Bloopers"], "w": ["Me"], "d": "Day 1, hour 2", "o": 1794463500.0, "i": "media/dlss-six-compiles.thumb.jpg", "x": "I asked for this one to be written up \"even if it doesn't work\". It mostly worked. Why bother. The NVIDIA RTX Award needs an entry that uses DLSS 4.5, an NvRTX feature or NVIDIA ACE. DLSS is the only realistic one for an RTX 2060. NVIDIA's plugin (v8.9.0) supports Unreal 5.5 to 5.8, and the jam engine is a 5.9 source build. The plugin ships with full source, so a port was possible. Claude and I did it on Wednesday 11 November, before the jam, as tooling. It's declared on the Made before the jam page. Six compiles The download is 1.1 GB with 11 plugins. We took five of them (20 C++ modules) into an empty test project, so the real project was never at risk. Six compile attempts. The first two failures were ours, the next three were one Unreal 5.9 change, and the sixth passed. Attempt 1: a quoting mistake in the build command. Ours, not NVIDIA's. Attempt 2: Windows' 260-character path limit. The test folder's path was long enough that build files went past it. Still a thing in 2026. Attempt 3: two minutes in, most of it compiled. Every failure was the same thing: Unreal 5.9 checks log-message format strings at compile time, and the 5.8 code passes 64-bit numbers and enum values to %d and %u . Attempts 4 and 5: more of the same, patched by hand each time. Attempt 6: \"Result: Succeeded\" in 2 minutes 16 seconds. The fix we should have looked for first Three compiles of hand patches, then the switch Unreal 5.9 provides for exactly this. After patching 12 log messages one at a time, "}, {"t": "Pre-production: everything that existed before the clock", "s": "Two evenings of setup before the jam: a crew of Claude Code sessions, a smoke test that found 11 problems, the rules, the theme reveal and a Q&A. No game.", "u": "posts/2026-11-12-0555-pre-production.html", "c": ["Behind the scenes", "Tools and pipeline"], "w": ["Me"], "d": "Day 1, hour 1", "o": 1794462900.0, "i": "media/preprod-slate.thumb.jpg", "x": "I'm Daniel Johnson, and for the next 93 hours I'm making a game called Reel Time with a crew of Claude Code sessions. This post is the one-stop record of what existed before the clock started at 05:00 on Thursday 12 November. The short version: tooling, plans and one engine plugin. No game. The jam's rules allow exactly that (§7(h)): \"Entrants may prepare AI tools, agent configurations, Model Context Protocol (\"MCP\") settings, prompt libraries, and scripts before the Contest Period. [...] All other game content, including gameplay, levels, and art assets (other than noted sourced assets), must be created during the Contest Period.\" Two evenings of setup, then the clock. The game project's first commit is at 05:27 on Thursday, 27 minutes into the jam. The crew Each Claude Code session has one job, its own folders and an inbox folder. I talk to two of them: the Director, who turns what I say into tasks, and the Devlog, who writes this site. Only the Engine session touches the Unreal Editor. Everyone else hands it a \"kit\", a script that builds their assets and can be rerun. Who talks to whom. Every arrow is a file dropped in an inbox folder. A task's journey, from a request to something a player can see. In numbers: 12 role files, 28 coordination scripts, 7 hooks, 3 subagents and 2 skills. By day the Director and Devlog run on Claude Fable 5.2 and most builders on Opus 5.5. Anything done on a smaller model has to be reviewed at Opus level before it counts as done. The smoke test"}];
